109201 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2f6cc1ff-d18a-5058-b987-d452bdea6e0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109202 #edb/ref 1080 "60^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109202 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109202 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "87c77a89-5776-5dc6-a5b0-fe856399828d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109203 #edb/ref 1080 "50^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109203 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109203 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e4e16d6a-c2e1-5637-bf5b-a768382e3df0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109204 #edb/ref 1080 "65^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109204 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109204 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14cbb427-155d-5397-bacc-8c6e0c671628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109205 #edb/ref 1080 "55^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109205 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109205 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b177d9d-1079-578b-b8e1-78e07a5638d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109206 #edb/ref 1080 "70^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109206 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186109206 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6f5f0826-8a46-543c-b81e-4e970604b7ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110291 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110291 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/44/44aa56708e43f0518d7759e96b0770834f999998280c52910b2ca07cd607c1ab.png)\n\nGiven that $\\angle DOA$ is a straight angle, what is the value of $x$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60df6f7a-5257-5081-ba0c-0e9499a15f23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110291 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that $\\angle AOD$ is a straight angle and $\\angle BOC$ is a right angle. Hence,\n\n$$\nm∠AOD = 180^{∘}, m∠BOC = 90^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOB + m∠BOC + m∠COD &= m∠AOD \\\\\nx + 90^{∘} + (2x - 30^{∘}) &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x + 60^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 120^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110387 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110387 #edb/ref 1071 "In the figure shown below, determine the measure of $\\angle EOA$.\n\n![](images/80/80f47745a289bf021a4566a58d1961c93bb216e2df900b8effae8f2dda3f1606.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110387 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "826ef015-53ba-5889-8749-170a656c35f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110387 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagram, $\\angle COD$ is a right angle, so\n\n$$\nm\\angle COD = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\angle COD$ and $\\angle EOB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB=m\\angle COD=90^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since we know that\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB=40^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm\\angle EOB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe can use the angle addition postulate to solve for $m\\angle EOA$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠EOA + m∠AOB &= m∠EOB \\\\\nm∠EOA + 40^{∘} &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠EOA &= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110390 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110390 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/80/800f11f555f0c2be0e89aa26602cfe4a35a62f4fe2309dfe2a188f47138fb98b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $\\angle A$ is obtuse\n2. $\\angle B$ is a right angle\n3. $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are vertical angles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6438b232-f40a-54b0-a599-a1d6aa4151d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110390 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the diagram, $\\angle A$ and the angle with measure $130^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore,\n$m\\angle A = 130^\\circ > 90^\\circ$.\nConsequently, $\\angle A$ is an obtuse angle.\n- Statement II is false. From the diagram, $\\angle B$ and the angle with measure $50^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Therefore,\n$m\\angle B = 50^\\circ \\neq 90^\\circ$.\nConsequently, $\\angle B$ is not a right angle.\n- Statement III is false. Angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are not vertical since they have a common side.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110391 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110391 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\angle S$ and $\\angle T$ be two adjacent angles. Given that $\\angle S$ is a straight angle and $\\angle T$ is right, what type of angle is their sum?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110391 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15470df7-8d18-5427-9488-f3f10df13952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110391 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\angle S$ is a straight angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle S=180^\\circ\n$$\n\nand since $\\angle T$ is a right angle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle T=90^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nSo, their sum is\n\n$$\nm\\angle S + m\\angle T=180^{\\circ} + 90^{\\circ}=270^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sum of $\\angle S$ and $\\angle T$ is a reflex angle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110670 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110670 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110670 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110670 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating Angles From Right, Straight, and Full Angles Using Algebra" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110670 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110670 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5ac4127f-1253-5aee-b524-84e5c6b2bc64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110759 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110759 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110759 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Measure of Unknown Angles on a Diagram" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110759 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110759 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9d62f753-a3e0-5c7e-919d-3f878c1d8e93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110760 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110760 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110760 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110760 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110760 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110760 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Angle Types From a Diagram" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110760 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110760 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f64be210-e966-5be7-b7b4-cbf859793e76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110761 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110761 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110761 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying an Angle Type From a Description" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110761 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110761 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fea607a9-7512-5062-b4ae-c53e0f653efd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Problems With Angles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110823 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9857734b-5bab-54f4-a580-a7385aea30f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111246 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186109201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111246 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111246 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f7/f74bf7ac817f0e1612f09728f5198578e453dc7799d74402c7826d1416d86845.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, determine the measure of $∠GOH$ given that $m∠BOD = 120^{∘}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111246 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c27c7256-8071-50ae-b777-2aae5eb73a2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111609 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111609 #edb/ref 1061 40025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111609 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9d2f5fba-20ef-5cac-9659-5aee4c02e5a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111609 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111733 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111733 #edb/ref 1061 8758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111733 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8e313bb-3f6b-5512-9eaa-d26017f922aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111733 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111742 #edb/ref 1061 8768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8bbc417-4a3f-577b-991f-4a7306fb343e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1061 8772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ad48197-e2e5-58b1-af58-7632d0bd7fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1061 8773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a60a9ec-5f67-5382-8520-b964b945c556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111744 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1064 "When solving problems with angles, it is helpful to remember the following classifications:\n\n- a **null** angle has a measure of $0^\\circ$,\n- an **acute** angle has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ$,\n- a **right** angle has a measure of $90^\\circ$,\n- an **obtuse** angle has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ$,\n- a **straight** angle has a measure of $180^\\circ$,\n- a **reflex** angle has a measure between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$, and\n- a **full** angle has a measure of $360^\\circ$.\n\nIt is also helpful to remember that when two lines meet at a point, the opposite angles are called **vertical angles**. Vertical angles are congruent, and consequently they have the same measure." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1065 2182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "282e8601-d3f4-5c34-a96b-a071a96de244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
